The REAL power of polymorphism is in RUNTIME polymorphism — it enables designing systems that are EXTENSIBLE without modification. Compile-time polymorphism (overloading) is convenient but doesn't enable extensibility.

WHY RUNTIME POLYMORPHISM IS USEFUL — four production benefits:

**Benefit 1: Eliminate if-else/switch chains**

```java
// (Avoid) WITHOUT polymorphism: growing if-else chain
public BigDecimal calculateFee(String accountType, BigDecimal amount) {
    if ("SAVINGS".equals(accountType)) {
        return amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.001"));
    } else if ("CURRENT".equals(accountType)) {
        return amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.002"));
    } else if ("NRI".equals(accountType)) {
        return amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.003"));
    } else if ("PREMIUM".equals(accountType)) {
        return BigDecimal.ZERO;  // premium: no fee
    }
    // Add 10 more types → 10 more else-if branches!
    // Adding FD account → MODIFY this method (breaks Open/Closed Principle)
    throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown type: " + accountType);
}

// (Recommended) WITH polymorphism: each type handles its own fee
public interface FeePolicy {
    BigDecimal calculateFee(BigDecimal amount);
}

public class SavingsFeePolicy implements FeePolicy {
    @Override public BigDecimal calculateFee(BigDecimal amount) {
        return amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.001"));
    }
}
public class PremiumFeePolicy implements FeePolicy {
    @Override public BigDecimal calculateFee(BigDecimal amount) {
        return BigDecimal.ZERO;
    }
}

// Usage: ONE line, no if-else!
BigDecimal fee = account.getFeePolicy().calculateFee(amount);
// JVM picks the correct implementation at runtime. ZERO if-else.
// Adding FD: create FdFeePolicy implements FeePolicy → ZERO changes to existing code!
```

**Benefit 2: Open/Closed Principle — open for extension, closed for modification**

```java
// The payment processor works with ANY PaymentChannel — existing or FUTURE:
public class PaymentProcessor {
    public void process(PaymentChannel channel, BigDecimal amount) {
        channel.debit(amount);      // polymorphic call
        channel.generateReceipt();  // polymorphic call
        channel.notifyCustomer();   // polymorphic call
    }
}
// Today: NeftChannel, UpiChannel, RtgsChannel
// Tomorrow: add CbdcChannel (digital rupee) → ZERO changes to PaymentProcessor!
// PaymentProcessor is CLOSED for modification, OPEN for extension.
```

**Benefit 3: Strategy pattern — swap behavior at runtime**

```java
// Interest calculation strategy — injected, not hardcoded:
public class AccountService {
    private final InterestStrategy interestStrategy;  // injected!

    public BigDecimal calculateInterest(Account account) {
        return interestStrategy.calculate(account);
        // At runtime: could be SimpleInterest, CompoundInterest, or TieredInterest
        // Swap strategy via configuration or A/B testing — zero code change!
    }
}
// In Spring: @Qualifier selects which strategy to inject.
```

**Benefit 4: Generic processing — one loop, many types**

```java
// Process ALL account types with ONE method:
public void processMonthEnd(List<Account> allAccounts) {
    for (Account account : allAccounts) {
        BigDecimal interest = account.calculateInterest();  // polymorphic!
        BigDecimal fee = account.calculateFee();              // polymorphic!
        account.applyMonthEnd(interest, fee);                 // polymorphic!
    }
    // SavingsAccount: 4% interest, 0.1% fee, credit interest
    // CurrentAccount: 0% interest, 0.2% fee, debit fee
    // NRIAccount: 3.5% interest, 0.3% fee, FEMA check
    // ONE loop. ZERO type checks. Fully extensible.
}
```

